======================================================================
(1) EVEN = p+q (Goldbach); ODD = p+2q (Lemoine, 2q=DOUBLED PRIME)
======================================================================
  even E : #Goldbach (p+q)        odd O : #Lemoine (p+2q), the doubled prime 2q
    E= 4:  1  e.g. (2, 2)
    E= 6:  1  e.g. (3, 3)
    O= 7:  1  e.g. p+2q = 3+4
    E= 8:  1  e.g. (3, 5)
    O= 9:  2  e.g. p+2q = 5+4
    E=10:  2  e.g. (3, 7)
    O=11:  2  e.g. p+2q = 7+4
    E=12:  1  e.g. (5, 7)
    O=13:  2  e.g. p+2q = 7+6
    E=14:  2  e.g. (3, 11)
    O=15:  2  e.g. p+2q = 11+4
    E=16:  2  e.g. (3, 13)
    O=17:  4  e.g. p+2q = 13+4
    E=18:  2  e.g. (5, 13)
    O=19:  2  e.g. p+2q = 13+6
    E=20:  2  e.g. (3, 17)
    O=21:  3  e.g. p+2q = 17+4
    E=22:  3  e.g. (3, 19)
    O=23:  3  e.g. p+2q = 19+4
    E=24:  3  e.g. (5, 19)
    O=25:  3  e.g. p+2q = 19+6
    E=26:  3  e.g. (3, 23)
    O=27:  4  e.g. p+2q = 23+4
    E=28:  2  e.g. (5, 23)
    O=29:  4  e.g. p+2q = 23+6
  => even covered by 2 odd primes; odd needs ONE doubled prime 2q (the even,
     parity-flipping summand). Doubled primes = parity-completion of the basis.

======================================================================
(2) A000568 via Burnside: EVEN cycles -> Fix=0; ALL-ODD -> 2^{pairs}
======================================================================
  n : A000568(n)   (computed: only ALL-ODD cycle types contribute; even cycles kill)
    3 : 2
    4 : 4
    5 : 12
    6 : 56
    7 : 456
    8 : 6880
  => the iso-class count is an 'all-odd-cycle' sum; the DOUBLING (an even cycle =
     antipodal-paired structure) annihilates Fix. Cycle-parity = the number-parity
     dictionary: odd cycles are the free/atomic contributors (like the odd primes).

======================================================================
(3) LRC DIMENSION TABLE: n -> n* -> channel type; DOUBLED PRIMES n=2p are clean
======================================================================
  n  parity   n*   n* type              n structure        clean prime channels?
   3  odd    3   PRIME               odd prime             YES
   4  even   2   PRIME               2*2 (DOUBLED PRIME)   YES
   5  odd    5   PRIME               odd prime             YES
   6  even   3   PRIME               2*3 (DOUBLED PRIME)   YES
   7  odd    7   PRIME               odd prime             YES
   8  even   4   prime-power 2^2     2*4 (2*{2: 2})        no
   9  odd    9   prime-power 3^2     odd comp {3: 2}       no
  10  even   5   PRIME               2*5 (DOUBLED PRIME)   YES
  11  odd   11   PRIME               odd prime             YES
  12  even   6   composite (CRT)     2*6 (2*{2: 1, 3: 1})  no
  13  odd   13   PRIME               odd prime             YES
  14  even   7   PRIME               2*7 (DOUBLED PRIME)   YES  <<< open
  15  odd   15   composite (CRT)     odd comp {3: 1, 5: 1}  no
  16  even   8   prime-power 2^3     2*8 (2*{2: 3})        no  <<< open
  17  odd   17   PRIME               odd prime             YES
  18  even   9   prime-power 3^2     2*9 (2*{3: 2})        no  <<< open
  19  odd   19   PRIME               odd prime             YES
  20  even  10   composite (CRT)     2*10 (2*{2: 1, 5: 1})  no
  21  odd   21   composite (CRT)     odd comp {3: 1, 7: 1}  no
  22  even  11   PRIME               2*11 (DOUBLED PRIME)  YES
  23  odd   23   PRIME               odd prime             YES
  24  even  12   composite (CRT)     2*12 (2*{2: 2, 3: 1})  no

  => clean PRIME channel modulus n* occurs iff n is an ODD PRIME or a DOUBLED PRIME
     n=2p. Among the open even cases: n=14=2*7 is a DOUBLED PRIME (n*=7 PRIME, clean,
     QR/Paley channels); n=16=2*8=2^4 and n=18=2*9=2*3^2 are 2x(prime power) ->
     n* a prime power -> FILTERED channels (S534, the messy ones). The doubled-prime
     structure of n controls the LRC channel cleanliness: n=14 is the easiest open even n.

======================================================================
SYNTHESIS: the factor of 2 (even prime) = DOUBLING = ANTIPODAL involution = the
universal PARITY operator. Doubled prime 2q = parity hinge (Lemoine, completes the
additive basis for ODD numbers). Even cycle = doubled odd cycle (antipodal) -> kills
Burnside Fix (A000568 = all-odd sum). LRC: n=2p doubled-prime dimensions inherit the
clean PRIME channel structure of p (n*=p); n=14 (=2*7) is the clean open even case.
ODD = atomic/free/prime; EVEN = doubled/antipodal; 2q & n=2p carry odd structure across.
======================================================================
